From c29bab61e667a070d3516325197fb5f7f9320d93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 18:37:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 29/35] windows: give WHP the 16-byte alignment its structs
 require
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`WinHvPlatformDefs.h` declares `WHV_UINT128` as `DECLSPEC_ALIGN(16)`, and
the alignment propagates to every type embedding it — `WHV_REGISTER_VALUE`
through `Reg128`, and `WHV_RUN_VP_EXIT_CONTEXT` through the six
`WHV_UINT128` XMM registers in `WHV_HYPERCALL_CONTEXT`.

windows-sys 0.61.2 emits both as plain `#[repr(C)]`. Their largest
primitive member is a `u64`, so Rust computes an alignment of 8. The sizes
are right (16 and 224, matching the header's own C_ASSERTs); only the
alignment is wrong, which is exactly the kind of mismatch that faults at
runtime instead of failing to compile.

`set_registers64` builds a `[WHV_REGISTER_VALUE; N]` on the stack and hands
its address to `WHvSetVirtualProcessorRegisters`. At align 8 that array
lands 8-mod-16 depending on the caller's frame layout, and WHP faults
dereferencing it — deterministically for a given call site, which is why
`setup_msrs` died on every boot while a standalone probe and the unit tests
doing the same thing from different frames passed.

Wrap both types in `#[repr(C, align(16))]` newtypes. A single-field newtype
keeps the size and field offsets, so an array of wrappers has exactly the
layout a C compiler gives the properly-aligned C type, and no copying or
allocation is needed anywhere.

The slice-taking `get_whp_registers`/`set_whp_registers` change their
element type rather than copying into an aligned scratch buffer: they run
on the emulator callback path, i.e. on every MMIO/PIO exit, where the
count is only known at runtime and a copy would mean a heap allocation per
exit. Making the alignment part of the type moves the requirement to where
it can be enforced. The emulator callbacks reborrow WHP's own buffers with
a checked cast, so a buffer that somehow was not aligned is reported as
E_INVALIDARG rather than turned into a reference.

Every `WHV_*` record windows-sys exposes was compared against the SDK
headers compiled for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc. Nine differ, all alignment
only, all descending from `WHV_UINT128`; the other seven are reachable
only as members of the two types wrapped here. Everything else this crate
passes across the FFI boundary matches the C layout exactly.

`const _: () = assert!(...)` guards pin the sizes and alignments — of the
wrappers and of the hand-rolled `WhvInterruptControl` — so a future
windows-sys bump that changes a layout fails the build instead of
producing another access violation months later.

The missing `align(16)` is a windows-sys metadata bug and is worth
reporting upstream.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
